--- body ---

## Context
Ticket 1216 projected `scripts/self-safe-pgrep.sh` as `~/.local/bin/pgrep`
and `~/.local/bin/pkill`, adding `-A` on procps-ng >= 4.0 so a waiting shell
cannot match or kill itself. The author finds a PATH-wide shim heavy-handed:
it changes the semantics of every program on PATH (`-A` also skips ancestors),
costs two `--version` runs per call, and procps offers no config or env
default to do it more narrowly. On padme the two links already point at
`/usr/bin` (observed 2026-10-28 18:53; cause not established).

All three runtimes already carry the PreToolUse guard
(`scripts/guard-destructive-bash.sh`: Claude hook, trusted Codex hook, Pi
`tool_call` extension) and every one honours a block (exit 2); only Claude
Code is known to support rewriting the command. So the portable fix is a
block on the agent's call, not a rewrite and not a PATH shim.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] The guard refuses `pgrep -f` / `pkill -f` (any spelling of `-f` /
      `--full`, combined short flags) lacking `-A` / `--ignore-ancestors`,
      with a reason naming `-A`, `'[p]attern'` and `timeout N tail --pid`
- [ ] Text mentions (quoted, heredoc) and non-`-f` calls are allowed
- [ ] `self-safe-pgrep.sh`, its two projections, its install/check probe and
      its tests are removed; `idh check` passes without them
- [ ] Docs propagated: guard header, README, `rules/coding-bash.md`,
      `adapters/README.md`
- [ ] `make check` passes
